DOCUMENT() ;
loadMacros(
	"PG.pl",
	"PGbasicmacros.pl",
	"PGchoicemacros.pl",
	"PGanswermacros.pl",
	"PGauxiliaryFunctions.pl",
	"PGgraphmacros.pl",
	"PGcomplexmacros.pl"
) ;
#sub i ();  #make i act like the imaginary unit.
$showPartialCorrectAnswers=1;
TEXT(beginproblem()) ;

$a =random(1,7,1) + non_zero_random(-7,7,1)*i;
$b = random(-7,-1,1) + non_zero_random(-7,7,1)*i;

$graph = init_graph(-8, -8, 8, 8,grid=>[16,16],axes=>[0,0]);
$point1 = closed_circle(Re($a), Im($a), 'red');
$point2 = closed_circle(Re($b), Im($b), 'blue');
$graph->stamps($point1,$point2);
BEGIN_TEXT
${BBOLD}The complex plane and polar form${EBOLD}
$PAR
\{ image(insertGraph($graph), width=>'200', height=>'200') \}
$BR
${BITALIC}(Click to enlarge.)${EITALIC}
$PAR
The red point represents the complex number \(z_1 =\)\{ans_rule(10)\}, 
$BR and the blue point represents the complex number \(z_2 = \)\{ans_rule(10) \}.
$PAR
Compute the moduli
$BR
\( |z_1| = \) \{ans_rule(10)\},
$BR
\( |z_2| = \) \{ans_rule(10)\}.
$PAR
Determine \(r\) and \(\theta\) (use an angle between \(-\pi\) and \(\pi\)) such that the red point \(z_1= re^{i\theta}\):
$BR
\(r\) =  \{ans_rule(10)\}
$BR
\(\theta\) =  \{ans_rule(10)\}
$PAR
Determine \(r\) and \(\theta\) (use an angle between \(-\pi\) and \(\pi\)) such that the blue point \(z_2= re^{i\theta}\):
$BR
\(r\) =  \{ans_rule(10)\}
$BR
\(\theta\) =  \{ans_rule(10)\}
END_TEXT

ANS cplx_cmp($a );
ANS cplx_cmp($b);
ANS num_cmp(abs($a));
ANS num_cmp(abs($b));
ANS num_cmp(abs($a));
ANS num_cmp(arg($a));
ANS num_cmp(abs($b));
ANS num_cmp(arg($b));

BEGIN_TEXT
$PAR
Using the law of exponents it is really easy to multiply complex numbers represented in polar coordinates -- the angles just add!  

\[ (r_1 e^{i\theta_1}) (r_2e^{i\theta_2}) = r_1r_2e^{i(\theta_1+\theta_2)}. \]

Determine \(z_1 \cdot z_2 = re^{i\theta}\) using polar coordinates and your answer above:
$BR
\(r\) =  \{ans_rule(10)\},
$BR
\(\theta\) =  \{ans_rule(10)\}.

$PAR

Check your answer by multiplying your \(a+bi\) expressions for \(z_1,z_2\) from the first part of this question, and then converting to polar coordinates. Can you plot this number on the graph?
END_TEXT
$result = $a*$b;
ANS num_cmp(abs($result));
ANS num_cmp(arg($result));

ENDDOCUMENT() ;       
